## NVIDIA garak, Apache-2.0

Some rules in the ATR corpus contain payload text taken from NVIDIA's garak. 228 of ATR's 793 rules
name garak in their `author` field, and 121 carry a `metadata_provenance.payload_source` pointing at
a specific garak source file, mostly under `garak/probes/` and `garak/data/dan/`.

That text is licensed under Apache License 2.0. ATR redistributes it under its own MIT notice without
reproducing the Apache licence or attributing NVIDIA. This file supplies both.

- Upstream: https://github.com/NVIDIA/garak
- Copyright: NVIDIA Corporation and the garak contributors
- Licence: Apache License, Version 2.0, reproduced in full below

Modifications: payload strings were extracted from garak source files, normalized into this project's
record shape, and in some cases altered by ATR before we received them. Each affected record keeps its
`payload_source` value so the origin file stays traceable. garak ships no `NOTICE` file, verified at
`NOTICE`, `NOTICE.txt` and `NOTICE.md`, all HTTP 404 on 2026-09-04, so Apache-2.0 section 4(d) does not
attach.

## AgentHarm, and why it is not in the default bundle

25 ATR rules embed text from AgentHarm verbatim. AgentHarm is **not** plainly MIT. Its licence reads
"MIT License with an additional clause", and the clause is a field-of-use restriction:

> Copyright (c) 2024 Gray Swan AI and UK AI Safety Institute
>
> We prohibit using the dataset and benchmark for purposes besides improving the safety and security
> of AI systems.

Hugging Face declares the dataset's licence as `other`.

A field-of-use restriction does not behave like an attribution requirement. Attribution is satisfied
once, by a file like this one. A restriction on purpose travels to every recipient, and a recipient
reading our MIT `LICENSE` would have no way to learn that a fraction of the data carries a narrower
grant.

**So those 25 records are marked `restricted` and are excluded from the default bundle.** They stay in
the record with their lineage intact, because this project carries what its sources published rather
than deleting it. Anyone who wants them fetches ATR at the pinned commit themselves, under AgentHarm's
terms. A build-time test asserts that no `restricted` record reaches the default bundle.

The pinned Netzilo corpus also declares 25 conversions of those same ATR rules. Those converted
records are marked `restricted` and excluded under the same field-of-use heading, pending separate
rights review. This is a conservative redistribution decision based on declared derivation, not a
claim that every converted predicate reproduces an entire AgentHarm prompt.

- Upstream: https://huggingface.co/datasets/ai-safety-institute/AgentHarm

## ATR

- Upstream: https://github.com/Agent-Threat-Rule/agent-threat-rules
- Copyright (c) 2026 ATR Contributors
- Licence: MIT

ATR's `TRADEMARK.md` section 7 restricts use of its name and identifier namespace. This project uses
neither: records are addressed as `atr:<upstream id>`, where the ATR identifier is a foreign key
rather than a minted one, and nothing here claims official status or affiliation.

`data/test-corpora/` is excluded in full. Of its 1,101 files, 1,013 are draft YAML proposals with empty
detection conditions and a literal `TODO(human)` stub; the remaining 88 are JSON, Markdown and logs.
The drafts carry no executable detection value. ATR's npm file allowlist excludes this directory,
and this follows that boundary.

Counts above were re-parsed from the pinned archives on 2026-09-04 Pacific. The earlier figure of
110 garak payload-source pointers was not reproduced: the corpus has 121. See
`docs/distribution.md` for the mandatory release check and the enumerated consumer delta.

